+// cSC4PlumbingSimulator — the water network. Recovered from Sim City 4 Deluxe Editionsub 1.2.1 (constructor 0x10009bc9e,
+// class id 0x0990c075, interface 0x47782ccc, object size 0x290; code 0x10009bc9e..0x1000a1290).
+//
+// What it does: every pipe occupant and every building that consumes (property 0xc8ed2d84) or produces (property
+// 0x88ed3303) water is entered in a per-cell flag map (+0xc8). A multi-step state machine (+0x128, one step per idle
+// message, see DoIdle) then re-computes everything after a change (+0x12c) or at the monthly tick (message 0x66956816):
+//   0 month start: ordinance factor, clear neighbour deals, zero the monthly counters
+//   1 UpdateWaterProduction: per producer production = capacity * funding * (power/pollution/source conditions)
+//   2 copy the pipe cells into the watered grid (value 8), reset the producer flags
+//   3 choose the next producer (or a neighbour import connection) and flood the pipe network from its cell (queue +0x68)
+//   4 spread the producer's output over the reached pipes and ring cells by breadth first search (queue +0x98): every
+//     reached cell is marked watered (bit 1), consuming cells (flag 0x40) draw BuildingConsumption * ordinance; export
+//     and import deals with neighbour cities are settled; then back to 3 for the next producer
+//   5 hand the watered state to the pipes and neighbour connections
+//   6 (time sliced) write the watered grid values (1 watered, 2 consumer, 4 producer-only) under every building and swap
+//     the two grids (+0x58 / +0x60; +0x60 is the published one)
+//   7 (time sliced) find the changed cell rectangle and invalidate the terrain view
+//   8 statistics: upkeep costs, ageing, history warehouse, status message, neighbour deals
+//   9 wait until something changed (+0x12c)
+//
+// Layout (original offsets in comments): +0x28 city, +0x30 message server, +0x38 occupant manager, +0x40 pollution simulator,
+// +0x48 neighbour simulator, +0x50 random, +0x58/+0x60 watered grids (uint8, tract size 1, instances 0x49d5bb8c / 0x49d5bba1),
+// +0xc8 cell flag map (width +0xd0, height +0xd4), +0xd8 visited bits (side +0xe0), +0xe4 month counter, +0xe8 average
+// pollution at the sources, +0xf0 budget department, +0xf8.. tunables, +0x110 producer list, +0x128 step, +0x1a8 current
+// producer, +0x1b0 cell scan iterator, +0x1c0/+0x1e0/+0x248/+0x268 response curves, +0x200 pipe list, +0x218.. pipe counts.
+//
+// Cell flags (+0xc8): 0x01 connects to x-1, 0x02 to z-1, 0x04 to x+1, 0x08 to z+1, 0x10 pipe, 0x20 producer lot, 0x40
+// consumer building. Watered grid values: 1 watered, 2 consumer, 4 (with 2 clear) producer cell, 8 pipe.
+//
+// Quirks kept: the flood fill queue stores one byte per coordinate (cities are at most 256 cells wide); the first lot of
+// a producer is looked up only at its reference cell; FillGridAtOccupant loops exclusive of the last cell when the cell has
+// no lot; DoBuildingChanged returns without releasing the building reference when the lot is missing (a leak the host
+// sees as an unbalanced AddRef); the producer flags +0x18/+0x19 are left uninitialised by the original constructors
+// (zeroed here); GetWaterProducerInfo keeps scanning after a match; random_shuffle of the pipes uses libc++'s forward
+// algorithm (host supplies the random index); the time slice check runs every iteration because the "history" step added
+// to its counter is 0x400.
+//
+// Services (city geometry, terrain, occupant/lot managers, pollution, budget, neighbours, history, messages, timer, resource
+// manager) are injected through IPlumbingHost; the original reaches them through the SL::sp* globals.
+#pragma once
+#include <cstdint>
+#include <deque>
+#include <list>
+#include <vector>
+
